Sure! Here's a simple JavaScript function that switches the order of adjectives in a sentence:
```javascript
function switchAdjectives(sentence) {
  const words = sentence.split(" ");
  const adjectives = [];

  for (let i = 0; i < words.length; i++) {
    if (words[i] === "beautiful" || words[i] === "green") {
      adjectives.push(words[i]);
    }
  }

  const reversedAdjectives = adjectives.reverse();
  const newSentence = "";

  for (let i = 0; i < words.length; i++) {
    if (adjectives.includes(words[i])) {
      newSentence += reversedAdjectives[i] + " ";
    } else {
      newSentence += words[i] + " ";
    }
  }

  return newSentence;
}

const originalSentence = "Beautiful green trees are in the park";
const newSentence = switchAdjectives(originalSentence);
console.log(newSentence); // Output: Green beautiful trees are in the park
```
This function splits the input sentence into an array of words, identifies the adjectives ("beautiful" and "green" in this case), reverses their order, and then builds a new sentence by combining the adjectives and non-adjective words in the correct order.

