Yes, when the server sets 'Access-Control-Allow-Origin' to '\*', it allows any origin to access the resource, including the client-side script that is making the request. In this case, the browser may not include the Authorization header in the request, as it considers the request to be allowed and does not need to authenticate the client.

To include the Authorization header in the request, you can use a library like `axios` or `fetch` with appropriate configuration options. For example, in `axios`, you can set the `headers` option to include the Authorization header:
```javascript
const axios = require('axios');

axios.get('https://example.com/api/resource', {
  headers: {
    Authorization: 'Bearer your_access_token',
  },
})
  .then(response => console.log(response))
  .catch(error => console.error(error));
```
In `fetch`, you can use the `Headers` object to include the Authorization header:
```javascript
const fetch = require('node-fetch');

const headers = new Headers();
headers.append('Authorization', 'Bearer your_access_token');

fetch('https://example.com/api/resource', {
  headers,
})
  .then(response => response.json())
  .then(data => console.log(data))
  .catch(error => console.error(error));
```
Make sure to replace `your_access_token` with the actual access token for the resource you are trying to access.

